# easydns_glue_record (Resource)

A glue record publishes IP addresses at the registry for a nameserver that lives inside the domain it serves. Without glue, `ns1.example.com` could not be resolved in order to answer for `example.com`.

{{tffile "examples/resources/glue_record/example_1.tf"}}

At least one of `ipv4` or `ipv6` is required. `host` must be inside `domain`; a host elsewhere is rejected during planning, because the registry only accepts glue from the domain that contains the host.

~> **Deletion is unverified and has failed in testing.** Against the EasyDNS sandbox, updating a glue record answers `HTTP 400: Error encountered attempting to modify glue record` and deleting one answers `HTTP 400: Error attempting to delete glue record. Please contact support for assistance (no result)`. A glue record created through this provider could be neither changed nor removed through it, so any change fails and `terraform destroy` leaves it at the registry. This may behave correctly in production; see the [verification status guide](../guides/verification-status). Create and read are verified.

## Registry propagation

`registry_configured` reports whether the registry has actually published the glue. A successful write does not mean the registry has caught up, so this attribute can be `false` immediately after an apply and `true` on a later refresh. If the status endpoint cannot be read, the provider warns rather than failing the apply.

## Deletion

A registry refuses to delete glue while any domain in the same TLD still delegates to the host. That refusal is surfaced as an error and is never retried. Move the delegation away first, then destroy the glue record.

## Import

```shell
terraform import easydns_glue_record.ns1 example.com:ns1.example.com
```

